evidence:
releaseSettlementHold.js:61-63:
  if (transaction.isDisputed && !transaction.disputeResolvedAt) {
    return { released: false, blockedBy: "DISPUTE" };
  }
and then, unconditionally, :74-84:
  await Transaction.updateOne({ _id: transactionId }, { $set: { settlementHold: false, settlementHoldReason: null, ... } });

The update carries no condition on who placed the hold. handleRazorpayWebhook.js:687+716 sets `isDisputed: false` and `disputeResolvedAt: new Date()` on WON/LOST/CLOSED, so after a LOST chargeback the guard at :61 evaluates false and the release goes through. The doc comment at :26-33 claims a dispute hold is "Released by an explicit admin action, never a webhook" — the code does not enforce that once the dispute is resolved.

settlementHold is the only gate for this row: helpers/settlements/settlementClaims.js:60-66 deliberately does NOT filter on isDisputed ("a chargeback we lost correctly sets it back to false"), and a chargeback never writes amountRefunded, so the `amountRefunded: { $lte: 0 }` filter at :74 does not block it either.

postRefundEntries.js:91-103 posts two rows against VENDOR_PAYABLE:
  { entryType: COLLECTION, amount: split.vendorClawback, direction: DEBIT }
  { entryType: VENDOR_PROMO_SHARE, amount: split.vendorPromoReversal, direction: CREDIT }

But calculateRefundSplit.js:167-169 already nets the promo reversal out of the clawback:
  let vendorClawback = round2(netBillRefund - vendorPromoReversal - commissionReversal);

So the vendor's promo share is given back twice: once by shrinking the DEBIT, once by the explicit CREDIT. The capture side (postCaptureEntries.js:59-67) credits netBill and debits vendorPromoCost, i.e. it does NOT pre-net anything — the two sides are not mirror images despite the doc table at postRefundEntries.js:19-25 claiming they are.

repairMissingHolds (refundJobs.js:175-186) is the whole repair:

  const result = await Transaction.updateMany(
    { _id: { $in: open.map((r) => r.transactionId) }, settlementHold: { $ne: true } },
    { $set: { settlementHold: true, settlementHoldReason: "Open refund — hold re-applied by reconcile" } },
  );

It sets the flag and stops. The normal path does two writes, not one — requestRefund.js:228 sets settlementHold, then requestRefund.js:247 calls taintSettlement({transaction, reason}). taintSettlement.js:9-19 states why: "`settlementHold` is only a **pre-claim** filter. Once `buildSettlements` has stamped `settlementId` on a transaction, setting the hold afterwards has no effect on that settlement at all." approveSettlement.js:92 is where the taint is enforced — its conditional filter carries `needsRevalidation: { $ne: true }`. repairMissingHolds imports no settlement helper at all (refundJobs.js:1-18).

buildSettlements.js:248-251
  const refundAdjustment = refunds.reduce(
    (sum, r) => sum + (Number(r.split?.vendorClawback) || 0), 0);
buildSettlements.js:258
  grossCollected - vendorPromoCost - commissionAmount - refundAdjustment

helpers/settlements/settlementClaims.js:128-137 (claimRefundAdjustments) claims EVERY COMPLETED refund for the brand, with no test of whether the underlying transaction was ever settled:
  { brandId, status: REFUND_REQUEST_STATUS.COMPLETED, settlementId: null, isDeleted: false }

Meanwhile the refunded transaction can never enter a settlement:
  helpers/refunds/applyRefundCompletion.js:118  $max: { amountRefunded: cumulative }
  helpers/refunds/applyRefundCompletion.js:133  settlementHold: true   // "Stays on."
and buildEligibilityFilter rejects both:
  settlementClaims.js:66  settlementHold: false
  settlementClaims.js:74  amountRefunded: { $lte: 0 }

The two quantities are identical by construction. computeTotals credits a settled txn (netBill - vendorPromoCost - commissionAmount); calculateRefundSplit.js:167-169 computes vendorClawback = netBillRefund - vendorPromoReversal - commissionReversal, which at ratio 1 is exactly that same figure.

RefundRequest.js:63-64 states the premise that makes this always-on: "Never recovered directly - the golden rule guarantees this money has not been paid out yet."

decideRefund.js:176-196 — rejectRefundAsVendor sets status VENDOR_REJECTED, isOpen: false, then calls releaseSettlementHold(). VENDOR_REJECTED is listed in REFUND_HOLD_RELEASING_STATUSES (constants/refund.js:82) as a state "where the money is not going anywhere".

But it is not terminal. executeRefund.js:38-41:
  const OVERRIDE_FROM = [REFUND_REQUEST_STATUS.VENDOR_REJECTED, REFUND_REQUEST_STATUS.VENDOR_TIMEOUT];
and approveRefundAsAdmin at :85-101 moves it to ADMIN_OVERRIDE with:
  $set: { status: ADMIN_OVERRIDE, ..., isOpen: true }
No settlementHold write, and no taintSettlement() call (contrast requestRefund.js:247 and handleRazorpayWebhook.js:729, both of which do taint). executeRefund (:209-255) then pays it out without ever checking whether the transaction has since been settled.

calculateRefundSplit.js:124,136-138,151-153,167-169 —
  const vendorSidePaid = round2(paid - feeActuallyPaid - taxOnTop);
  const convenienceFeeRefund = isFullRefund ? feeActuallyPaid : 0;
  const vendorPortion = round2(totalRefund - convenienceFeeRefund - taxRefund);
  const ratio = vendorSidePaid > 0 ? vendorPortion / vendorSidePaid : 0;
  const netBillRefund = round2(netBill * ratio);
  let vendorClawback = round2(netBillRefund - vendorPromoReversal - commissionReversal);

`ratio` is computed against the WHOLE vendor side on every partial, never against the vendor side still unrefunded, and is never capped at 1. assertBalances (line 217-255) cannot catch it: it only checks vendorClawback+commissionReversal-platformPromoReversal === vendorPortion, which holds by construction.

refundJobs.js:167-186 —
  const open = await RefundRequest.find({ isOpen: true, isDeleted: false })
    .select("_id transactionId")
    .limit(500)
    .lean();
  ...
  const result = await Transaction.updateMany(
    { _id: { $in: open.map((r) => r.transactionId) }, settlementHold: { $ne: true } }, ...

The `find` does not filter on "hold actually missing" and has no sort or cursor — the missing-hold predicate lives only in the updateMany. Its own comment (line 157-165) calls this "the guarantee this whole design rests on".

services/refunds/decideRefund.js:16-20 imports `sendQuietly`, `notifyCustomerRefundApproved`, `notifyCustomerRefundRejected` — and calls none of them. `approveRefundAsVendor` ends at recordClaimHistory + `return present(updated)` (lines 139-156); `rejectRefundAsVendor` ends the same way (lines 198-214); `cancelRefund` likewise (lines 270-281). A grep for `sendQuietly(` in the file returns only the import on line 17.

Meanwhile services/refunds/executeRefund.js:118-128:
  /** Only on an **override**. On the normal path the vendor's approval already
   * told the customer their money is coming ... */
  if (isOverride) { await sendQuietly(() => notifyCustomerRefundApproved({ request: updated }), ...); }

Both notification functions exist and work — helpers/notifications/refundNotices.js:174 and :219 — and notifyCustomerRefundRejected is wired only into rejectRefundAsAdmin (executeRefund.js:179-182).

services/refunds/refundJobs.js:167-171:
  const open = await RefundRequest.find({ isOpen: true, isDeleted: false })
    .select("_id transactionId")
    .limit(500)
    .lean();

No sort and no cursor. Unlike escalateStaleRefunds (refundJobs.js:73-92), which mutates `status` and so drains its own batch, this job never changes `isOpen` — the matched set is identical on every run, so the same first 500 documents are re-examined every 30 minutes (jobs/index.js:161-164) and everything past position 500 is never read. The doc block above it (refundJobs.js:154-166) states the reason it repairs rather than reports: "the window between noticing and fixing is a settlement run."

releaseJobLock (jobLock.js:135-165) builds `update.$set = { lockedBy: null, expiresAt: now, ... }` and writes it with a filter that names only the job:

    await JobLock.updateOne({ _id: name }, update, { upsert: true });

The comment at 162-164 justifies the unscoped filter for the *health* fields ("the health fields must land either way") but the same $set also carries `lockedBy` and `expiresAt`. Contrast renewJobLock (jobLock.js:81), which is correctly scoped: `{ _id: name, lockedBy: INSTANCE_ID }`. Losing the lease mid-run is also non-fatal: startJobLockHeartbeat (jobLock.js:107-111) only console.warns and the run continues unlocked.

sweepAbandonedDrafts skips any draft holding rows (settlementJobs.js:320-324):

    const holds = await Transaction.countDocuments({ settlementId: draft._id });
    if (holds > 0) continue;

and alertLateSettlements cannot see it either, because its filter requires money on the row (settlementJobs.js:155):

    netPayable: { $gt: 0 },

while models/Settlement.js:88 declares `netPayable: { type: Number, default: 0 }` and buildSettlements only writes the totals *after* the claim (buildSettlements.js:172-188). DRAFT is in SETTLEMENT_PRE_PAYOUT_STATUSES (constants/settlement.js:184) so the status half of the filter matches — netPayable is what excludes it.

settlementJobs.js:167-187 claims the row first, sends second, and the send cannot fail loudly:

    const claimed = await Settlement.findOneAndUpdate(
      { _id: settlement._id, overdueAlertsSent: settlement.overdueAlertsSent || 0 },
      { $set: { overdueAlertsSent: (settlement.overdueAlertsSent || 0) + 1 } },
      { returnDocument: "after" },
    ).lean();
    if (!claimed) continue;
    await sendQuietly(() => notifyAdminSettlementLate({ ... }), "admin settlement late");
    alerted += 1;

sendQuietly (helpers/notifications/sendQuietly.js:12-19) catches and returns null. `alerted` is incremented regardless of whether anything was delivered. The selection filter is `overdueAlertsSent: { $lt: 1 }` (settlementJobs.js:157), so once the counter is 1 the settlement is never selected again. The same order appears in remindVendorsAboutRefunds (refundJobs.js:361-372).

refundJobs.js:168-171:

    const open = await RefundRequest.find({ isOpen: true, isDeleted: false })
      .select("_id transactionId")
      .limit(500)
      .lean();

No sort, and nothing in the loop changes `isOpen`, so the set is not drained by the sweep. A FAILED refund stays in it for ever — refundJobs.js:263-274 writes `status: FAILED` together with `isOpen: true` and the comment at 272 confirms it is deliberate ("Still open, and the hold stays on"). Only an admin re-decision or a successful execution closes one.

claimJobs.js:163-170:

    const stranded = await Transaction.find({
      ...buildTransactionFilter({ purpose: TRANSACTION_PURPOSE.VOUCHER_CLAIM, verified: true }),
      settlementStage: { $exists: true, $ne: SETTLEMENT_STAGE.COMPLETE },
      verifiedAt: { $lte: cutoff },
    }).limit(50);

No sort, no skip, and a row is removed from the set only by reaching SETTLEMENT_STAGE.COMPLETE. The loop at 177-194 catches every error, increments `failed`, and leaves the row exactly as it was — there is no attempt counter, no backoff, and no quarantine. There is also no index for this shape: models/Transaction.js:341-464 has nothing on settlementStage (the closest, `{purpose:1, status:1, createdAt:-1}` at line 456, does not cover it), so the query is a collection scan every 15 minutes.

refundJobs.js:167-186:
  const open = await RefundRequest.find({ isOpen: true, isDeleted: false })
    .select("_id transactionId")
    .limit(500)
    .lean();
  ...
  await Transaction.updateMany({ _id: { $in: open.map(r => r.transactionId) }, settlementHold: { $ne: true } }, { $set: { settlementHold: true, ... } });
No sort, no cursor, no continuation — the same first 500 rows in natural (roughly insertion) order every run, every 30 minutes (jobs/index.js:161-164).

The open set is not self-draining: constants/refund.js:63-65 keeps FAILED in REFUND_OPEN_STATUSES on purpose ("the money still has to go back"), and getPaymentHealth.js:172-176 counts FAILED refunds older than a day as a known CRITICAL backlog with no automated resolution until MANUAL_BANK exists.

requestRefund.js:157-164 —
  const requestedAmount =
    amount === undefined || amount === null
      ? Math.round((paidAmount - alreadyRefunded) * 100) / 100
      : amount;
  if (!refundConfig.allowPartial && requestedAmount < paidAmount - alreadyRefunded) {
    throwError(422, "Partial refunds are not available. Please request the full amount.");
  }

The left side is rounded to 2dp; the right side is the raw float. calculateRefundSplit.js:93 then caps at the rounded ceiling:
  if (totalRefund > refundableCeiling + PAISA) throwError(422, `Only ₹${refundableCeiling.toFixed(2)} …`);

alreadyRefunded > 0 is reachable even with allowPartial false: approveRefundAsVendor (decideRefund.js:88-100) lets a vendor approve LESS than requested and has no allowPartial check.

requestRefund.js:204-219 —
  if (error?.code === DUPLICATE_KEY) {
    const existing = await RefundRequest.findOne({ transactionId: transaction._id, isOpen: true }).lean();
    if (existing) return present(existing, { reused: true });
  }
  throw error;

No comparison against the amount/reason the caller just asked for. present() (line 294-305) returns `amount: request.requestedAmount` — the OLD request's figure — and controllers/refunds/raise.js:11 sends it as HTTP 201 "Refund request raised successfully." The `split` computed at line 167 for the new amount is discarded.

The justification at line 208-210 assumes "a second tap, or a refreshed page"; nothing enforces that assumption.

applyRefundCompletion.js:60-83 sets isOpen:false on the RefundRequest, releasing the partial unique index. Two awaited round trips follow (line 90-93: Transaction.findById + VoucherClaim.findById) before line 112-118 finally writes the new total:
  $max: { amountRefunded: cumulative },

requestRefund.js:149-150 reads the ceiling from that same field:
  const paidAmount = transaction.paidAmount ?? transaction.amount;
  const alreadyRefunded = transaction.amountRefunded || 0;

decideRefund.js:104-110 recomputes the split at approval time from the THEN-current transaction.amountRefunded, and calculateRefundSplit.js:93 refuses anything over the ceiling.

services/refunds/refundJobs.js:317-318 reads the setting live:
  const windowHours = Number(config.refund?.vendorApprovalHours) || 24;
then at 349-353 mixes it with the stored deadline:
  const remaining = new Date(request.vendorRespondBy).getTime() - now;
  const elapsedFraction = 1 - remaining / (windowHours * HOUR_MS);
  const due = marks.filter((mark) => elapsedFraction >= mark).length;

`vendorRespondBy` is written once from the setting in force at creation (requestRefund.js:180, 202). The same file's own doc block, refundJobs.js:29-36, states the deadline is stored precisely so that "raising the setting tomorrow silently extend[s] every request already waiting" cannot happen — this function reintroduces exactly that coupling.

